<Page
    x:Class="MbcsCentral.Help.QueryEditor"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:local="clr-namespace:MbcsCentral.Help"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    Title="QueryEditor"
    Height="1200"
    mc:Ignorable="d">
    <Page.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <ResourceDictionary Source="HelpStyles.xaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Page.Resources>

    <Grid>
        <ScrollViewer
            Width="Auto"
            Height="643"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            CanContentScroll="True"
            VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <StackPanel
                Height="640"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Top">
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource HeaderText}">Query Editor</TextBlock>
                <Separator />
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerTitleText}" Text="RULES" />
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerText}">1. TABLE Names</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">Must be surrounded by square brackets i.e. [TableName].</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerText}">2. COLUMN Names</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">If column name contains spaces, must be surrounded by square brackets i.e. [ColumnName].</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerText}">3. Parameters (search criteria)</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    A parameter is a value in a WHERE clause that needs to be entered by the user to filter the results.<LineBreak />
                    The parameter is given a name that starts with an at sign, @, and includes no spaces.<LineBreak />
                    For example if you want to restrict a query to return results for a single building as in the example query, use a phrase such as<Bold>pm_Tenants.[Building Number] = @BuildingNumber.</Bold><LineBreak />
                    The value for<Bold>BuildingNumber</Bold>
                    will be prompted when the query is run.</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerText}">4. SUBQUERIES that can be reused</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><Bold>A.</Bold>
                    Create as separate, named queries</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><Bold>B.</Bold>
                    In main query, create a temporary table to contain results of subquery prior to body of main query.</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><Bold>C.</Bold>
                    EXEC subquery.</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><Bold>D.</Bold>
                    In main query, refer to temporary table where the subquery is used.</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><Bold>E.</Bold>
                    Drop temporary table after main query.</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><LineBreak /><Italic>For Example if the query that you want to use contains a subquery (in bold) is</Italic><LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <Border
                    Margin="5"
                    BorderBrush="Blue"
                    BorderThickness="1">
                    <TextBlock
                        Width="Auto"
                        Margin="5"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
                        SELECT<LineBreak />
                        pm_Tenants.Apartment, pm_Tenants.Rooms, pm_Tenants.Type, pm_Tenants.[Tenant Name], [Rent] + COALESCE([AMOUNT],0) AS [MONTHLY RENT], pm_Tenants.[Lease Expiration Date]<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        FROM<LineBreak />
                        pm_Tenants<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        LEFT JOIN<LineBreak /><Bold>(SELECT pm_Charges.[Sequence Number], pm_Charges.[Charge Code], pm_Charges.Amount, pm_Charges.[Starting Date] FROM pm_Charges WHERE [pm_Charges].[Charge Code] = '9' AND [pm_Charges].[Starting Date] &gt; GetDate() ) As t</Bold><LineBreak />
                        ON<LineBreak /> <LineBreak />
                        pm_Tenants.[Sequence Number] = t.[Sequence Number]<LineBreak />
                        WHERE (((pm_Tenants.[Building Number])=@BuildingNumber))<LineBreak />
                        ORDER BY pm_Tenants.[Tenant Number]</TextBlock>
                </Border>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><LineBreak /><Italic>
                        The subquery, in bold, should be created as a separate query which can than be referenced by any other query.
                        <LineBreak />
                        Let's call that newly created subquery: SORTED_CHARGES.
                    </Italic><LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                    <Italic>The main query would be rewritten as (numbers in bolded parens are for reference only):</Italic><LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <Border
                    Margin="5"
                    BorderBrush="Blue"
                    BorderThickness="1">
                    <TextBlock
                        Width="Auto"
                        Margin="5"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                        TextWrapping="Wrap"><Bold>(1)</Bold>
                        IF OBJECT_ID('#Temp', 'U') IS NOT NULL<LineBreak /><Bold /> <Bold /> <Bold /> <Bold /> <Bold />
                        DROP TABLE [#Temp];<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        <Bold>(2)</Bold>
                        CREATE TABLE [#Temp]<LineBreak />
                        ([Sequence Number] int, [Charge Code] varchar(2), Amount money, [Starting Date] datetime );<LineBreak />
                        INSERT INTO [#Temp]<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        <Bold>(3)</Bold>
                        EXEC SORTED_CHARGES;<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        SELECT<LineBreak />
                        pm_Tenants.Apartment, pm_Tenants.Rooms, pm_Tenants.Type, pm_Tenants.[Tenant Name], [Rent] + COALESCE([AMOUNT],0) AS [MONTHLY RENT], pm_Tenants.[Lease Expiration Date]<LineBreak />
                        FROM pm_Tenants<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        <Bold>(4)</Bold>
                        LEFT JOIN [#Temp]<LineBreak />
                        ON<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        pm_Tenants.[Sequence Number] = [#Temp].[Sequence Number] WHERE (((pm_Tenants.[Building Number])=@BuildingNumber))
                        ORDER BY pm_Tenants.[Tenant Number]<LineBreak /><LineBreak />
                        <Bold>(5)</Bold>
                        DROP TABLE [#Temp];</TextBlock>
                </Border>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerTitleText}"><LineBreak />
                    EXAMPLE NOTES:<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    The temporary table is created first. Temporary tables can have almost any name, we are using, 'Temp'.  Temporary tables have a pound sign (#) prepended to them.<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    To avoid errors, the query makes sure that if the temporary table exists it is first deleted<Bold>(1)</Bold>
                    , then it is created<Bold>(2)</Bold>
                    . The temporary table must have columns that exactly math the order and data type of the columns in subquery's SELECT statement.<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    The subquey is then executed<Bold>(3)</Bold>
                    . This fills the temporary table with the result set returned by the subquery.<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    The bolded subquery in the original main query is simply replaced by the name of the temporary table<Bold>(4)</Bold>
                    .<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">
                    Finally, the temporary table should be dropped<Bold>(5)</Bold>
                    .</TextBlock>
                <Border
                    Width="Auto"
                    Margin="5"
                    BorderBrush="Blue"
                    BorderThickness="2" />
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource InnerTitleText}">ADDITIONAL NOTES:</TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}"><LineBreak />
                    SQL will be syntactically validated when it is saved.  You can also click on the VALIDATE button at any time while you are editing the query.<LineBreak /></TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource IndentedInnerText}">While it is important that the query be syntactically accurate, that is no guartanee that it will perform as expected.  Only trying it in the Database Queries form and examining the results will tell you that.</TextBlock>
            </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>
    </Grid>
</Page>
